# Shell scripts must keep LF endings.
#
# The container runner (`scripts/run-specs-in-container.sh`) is executed by
# bash inside the Linux Playwright image. With Git's default
# `core.autocrlf` behaviour on Windows, a committed `.sh` file is checked
# out with CRLF line endings, and bash then fails on the shebang line:
#
#   /tmp/scripts/run-specs-in-container.sh: /bin/bash^M: bad interpreter
#
# Pinning LF here makes the checkout platform-independent.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The baseline-regeneration workflow and the mockup specs read the PNG
# baselines as binary. Never let Git attempt a line-ending conversion on
# them — a converted PNG is a different file, and the visual diff would
# fail for a reason that has nothing to do with the UI.
*.png binary

# Memory-bank markdown must keep LF endings.
#
# The specsmd engine's `bolt-complete.cjs` extracts YAML frontmatter with
# the regex `/^---\n([\s\S]+?)\n---/`, which requires a bare `\n`. With
# Git's default `core.autocrlf` behaviour on Windows, a committed `.md`
# file is checked out with CRLF line endings, the regex does not match,
# and the completion gate dies with "Invalid bolt file (no frontmatter)".
#
# The same failure hits every CRLF-checked-out memory-bank file, whether
# it was written by hand or by the engine itself.
*.md text eol=lf
